{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["And the reason for <b>only against YHWH do not rebel</b> [is] that your fear of the strength of the people dwelling in it constitutes rebellion against the honored Name, for it was not by your own strength that you came forth from Egypt, but the hand of YHWH did wonders with you, and He promised you that He would dispossess them before you — if so, believe and you will succeed. And they said: 'And you, do not fear, etc., for they are our bread,' for even by the nature and custom of the world they will fall before us, for our terror has fallen upon them and they will let us devour them like bread." "<b>Their shadow has departed from over them</b> — their shield and their strength; the mighty among them have died. Another interpretation: the shadow of the Place [i.e., HaShem] has departed from over them — this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Numbers 14:9). And Rabbi Abraham [Ibn Ezra] said that <b>their shadow (צִלָּם)</b> is a shield and buckler with which they cast a shadow over the warriors; they say that our terror has fallen upon them, and they will not take shield or buckler or helmet to arm themselves to fight against us. And his explanation is fine. But it is possible that Scripture alludes to what is known: on the night of the sealing [of the decree], there will be no shadow over the head of the man who is to die in that year; therefore it says, their shadow has already departed from over them — for death has been decreed upon them — and YHWH is with us, for He is the One who dwells in our midst and performs miracles and wonders for us before the eyes of all who see; therefore do not fear them. Or it may allude to the celestial princes, for no nation falls until its prince falls first (Mekhilta Shirata 2), as it is written: 'YHWH will punish the host of the high ones on high,' and afterwards, 'and on the kings of the earth on the earth' (Isaiah 24:21), and as explained in the book of Daniel (10:20). It says: the power through which the nations live has already departed, and YHWH is the One who humbles them with us; therefore do not fear them. And so they said in the midrash on Song of Songs: 'the shadows flee away' (Song of Songs 2:17) — these are the princes of the nations and their angels, for they are the shadow over the nations. And I have already mentioned this in other places (Leviticus 18:25; above on 11:15)."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {1 "Ramban cites two earlier verse references in the parenthetical at the end; the first (Leviticus 18:25) is to his commentary on that verse, the second (above on 11:15) is to his earlier commentary on Numbers 11:15 in this same work. The citation of Mekhilta Shirata is by chapter name and number."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:05:43.485480456Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}